/**
@page exa_parabolic_indenter Parabolic indenter contact

The example presses a deformable parabolic top body into a flat deformable
bottom body in a unit square. Both bodies use Young's modulus 1000, Poisson's
ratio zero, bilinear displacement interpolation on a 10 by 10 background mesh,
Nitsche displacement boundary conditions, and ghost stabilization. Contact is
integrated on both nonconformal interface orientations. Runs require ArborX
and currently use one MPI rank.

The original linear-contact and nonlinear Mlika cases retain their stored reference
checks and their CTest registration, Parabolic_Indentor-1-procs. Mlika implements
only the frictionless energy formulation of thesis Eqs. (4.28)-(4.30); frictional
contact uses Seitz. The existing Mlika four-scalar input layout is retained:
nonpositive frictionless marker, reserved value, penalty-only flag, debug flag.
Positive friction coefficients are rejected. The former dormant penalty-friction
prototype and its traction-history map have been removed.

@section indenter_seitz Seitz variants

The nonlinear input also supports Seitz's unbiased unsymmetric Nitsche
formulation with compressible Neo-Hookean materials in plane strain.
Driver globals select the contact law before the input library
loads; the input supplies its own defaults for standalone use. The Seitz
variants use the same mesh, material, stabilization and solver tolerances.
The top boundary moves horizontally by 0.03 and vertically by -0.051 over
three physical steps ending at time 10. Each step uses the existing nonlinear
load continuation and ray-remapping strategy. This loading sequence exercises
previous-slab displacement contributions to the slip increment. The friction
projection uses current constitutive traction; no traction history is stored.

- Seitz-Frictionless uses a negative friction coefficient to select frictionless
  contact.
- Seitz-Friction uses Coulomb coefficient 0.3; the tangential penalty is derived
  from the contact stabilization parameter.
- Seitz-FrictionlessSensitivity and Seitz-FrictionSensitivity repeat the
  corresponding model with the top Young's modulus as one constant property
  ADV. The bottom modulus is fixed. The sweep checks final-state top-body
  vertical and horizontal displacement integrals against full-workflow
  forward, backward and central finite differences at two step sizes.

Each case is an explicit Catch test with its own CTest working directory.
Sensitivity partials use MORIS's semi-analytical quadrature machinery, while
current-state and previous-state contact Jacobians are analytical. The
sensitivity cases perform a complete backward solve through the load history.
The example does not exercise shape sensitivities or parallel contact.

@section indenter_checks Result checks

The Seitz checkers read the VIS Exodus file and check mesh dimensions, node
and element counts, reference-node coordinates, final time, displacement and
global displacement integrals. Time_Offset is zero in Seitz cases, so output
uses a stable .exo filename and physical time during sensitivity sweeps.
Reference data and their acquisition are
recorded beside the checker. Set gPrintReferenceValues in the driver to print
reference values. Sensitivity cases additionally check the sweep HDF5 data;
each run removes its previous checked outputs before starting.
*/
